00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:46.929
That actually turned into a very painful pratfall.

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:47.999
That better have been funny.

00:00:47.999 --> 00:00:49.510
Right, anyway, I really liked your video about
prisons.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.809
It spurred some interesting discussions, and
I wanted to add a couple things.

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:55.010
First, there was a lot of talk in comments
about the role of race in sentencing, and

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.499
the data there is pretty overwhelming in the
U.S. A 2013 study by the U.S. Sentencing Commission

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:04.269
found that African-Americans on average received
sentences 20% longer than white people who

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.689
committed similar crimes.

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:09.060
Also, they found that that disparity is actually
increasing.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.280
Socio-economic class is also a big factor
in sentencing, and the question of length

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.690
of sentence brings me to one other point I
want to make.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:18.640
Per capita, a bunch of European countries
actually admit more people to prison per year

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.420
than the US does, but their sentences are
much shorter; and that's one of the big reasons

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.000
we have the highest incarceration rate in
the world.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.170
It isn't that we sentence so many people to
prison, it's that we sentence them for so

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.170
long.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.100
And in the US that's driven largely by long
sentences for drug offences.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.490
I've put some links in the doobilydoo for
those who are interested.

